   "*For* and *since* with the present perfect mean the situation continues. *I lived there for ten years* means you left.",
   "British English uses the present perfect more than American English. Both are correct; be consistent."
  ],
+ "contraste": {
+  "es": "Spanish uses he comprado for finished past time in much of Spain, and the simple past almost always in Latin America. Neither maps onto English: the English rule is about whether the time period is finished, not about how recent the event was.",
+  "de": "German uses the Perfekt as the normal spoken past for finished events, which produces I have bought it yesterday. In the other direction, German uses the present with seit for situations that are still going on (Ich wohne seit 2020 hier), which produces I live here since 2020 instead of I have lived here since 2020.",
+  "it": "In northern and standard spoken Italian, the passato prossimo is used for finished events (ieri ho comprato una macchina), which produces I have bought a car yesterday. Italian also uses the present with da for ongoing situations (vivo qui da due anni), which leads to I live here since two years.",
+  "fr": "The passé composé is the normal spoken past in French (hier j'ai acheté une voiture), which produces I have bought a car yesterday. French also uses depuis + present for ongoing situations (j'habite ici depuis deux ans), which leads to I live here since two years instead of I have lived here for two years.",
+  "pt": "Portuguese uses the simple past for finished events (comprei um carro ontem), which matches the English past simple well. The Portuguese pretérito perfeito composto (tenho feito) means something repeated up to now and corresponds more closely to the present perfect continuous (I have been doing); ongoing situations with há (moro aqui há dois anos) lead to I live here for two years."
+ },
  "errores": [
