+  "de": "German sagen covers both say and tell (Er hat mir gesagt…), which leads to he said me. In addition, German marks reported speech with the Konjunktiv I and does not backshift, so German speakers often keep the original tense: She said she is tired.",
+  "it": "Italian dire covers both say and tell (mi ha detto che…), which leads to he said me. Backshift is familiar, because Italian also shifts tenses in reported speech (ha detto che era stanco).",
+  "fr": "French dire covers both say and tell (il m'a dit que…), which leads to he said me. Backshift is familiar from the French concordance des temps (il a dit qu'il était fatigué), so the tense changes transfer well.",
+  "pt": "Portuguese dizer covers both say and tell (disse-me que…), which leads to he said me; contar is used for telling a story, which covers only part of tell. Backshift is familiar, since Portuguese also shifts tenses in reported speech."
+ },
  "errores": [
