+ "contraste": {
+  "es": "Spanish has gerund and participle clauses too, but tolerates a dangling subject far more readily (estando en Madrid, me robaron). English does not: the same construction reads as an error.",
+  "de": "German compresses relative clauses into participles placed before the noun (die mit dem Medikament behandelten Patienten), while English places them after it (the patients given the drug). German speakers therefore know the compression but have to move the whole participle phrase behind the noun.",
+  "it": "Reduced relatives are easy, because Italian uses exactly the same postnominal participle (i pazienti trattati con il farmaco – the patients treated with the drug). The risk lies in participle and gerund clauses: colloquial Italian tolerates a different subject (essendo a Madrid, mi hanno derubato), while English treats this as an error.",
+  "fr": "Reduced relatives are easy, since French uses the same postnominal participle (les patients traités avec le médicament). French school grammar also insists that the gérondif shares the subject of the main clause, so French speakers already know the rule that prevents dangling participles in English.",
+  "pt": "Reduced relatives are easy, since Portuguese uses the same postnominal participle (os doentes tratados com o fármaco). Gerund clauses with a different subject are common, especially in Brazilian Portuguese, so dangling participles are the main risk."
+ },
  "errores": [
   "*Walking into the room, the lights were off* — the lights were not walking.",
   "Reducing where the pronoun is the object: *the book read*.",
